lsewhere", "Independent Chris Getty and ballot-access attacks could shave the margin"], "rationale": "The district's deep Democratic lean and a favorable national environment make Patty Garcia a heavy favorite in this open seat, and the lone poll plus prediction markets point the same way. An independent drawing local support and dated polling keep the expected margin more modest than the raw district numbers alone would suggest.", "watch": "If Chris Getty consolidates anti-Garcia voters or the ballot-access controversy escalates, the margin could tighten, though a Republican win would require a historic shift.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Newest usable poll is 85 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Chuy García (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "This open Chicago-area seat sits in territory that has leaned Democratic by about 17 points, and the national environment adds to that advantage. Patty Garcia, positioned as retiring incumbent Chuy Garcia's successor, has raised over half a million dollars, while Republican Lupe Castillo has shown little visible fundraising. An August survey of registered voters put Garcia ahead 34 to 15, though half of respondents were undecided or backing others.\n\nIndependent Chris Getty, with more than $400,000 raised, is competing for votes and has clashed with Garcia at candidate forums, and rivals have pressed her over ballot-access moves. Prediction markets still price the Democratic side near 98 percent. District lean, environment, money, and market pricing all point to a comfortable Democratic hold, with the open question being the size of the margin rather than the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9927, "margin": 21.92}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9929, "margin": 22.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9941, "margin": 22.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9922, "margin": 22.0}, {"